.TH PRX 1 "" "prx 1.0.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
prx \- user-space container and path-translation engine
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B prx
.I command
[\fIoptions\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B prx
runs a glibc-based Linux root filesystem as an unprivileged user, without
root, namespaces or ptrace. Paths are translated by an
.B LD_PRELOAD
shim and the guest's dynamic loader and libc are patched at run time.
.PP
.B prx
is a convenience tool, not a security boundary: a program that issues raw
syscalls or is statically linked can bypass the translation layer.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B build
Compile the C runtime shim and loader patcher (needs a C compiler).
.TP
\fBinstall\fR \fINAME\fR \fIFILE|URL\fR [\fB\-\-sha256\fR \fIHEX\fR]
Extract a rootfs archive as container NAME. With
.B \-\-sha256
the archive is verified before extraction; without it a warning is printed.
.TP
\fBlogin\fR \fINAME\fR [\fIoptions\fR] [\fB\-\-\fR \fIcommand\fR...]
Start a shell or command inside the container.
.RS
.TP
\fB\-b\fR, \fB\-\-bind\fR \fIPATH\fR | \fIHOST\fR:\fIGUEST\fR
Bind a host path into the guest. Paths must be shorter than 1024 bytes and
must not contain
.B :
or
.BR = ;
at most 50 binds can be given. Invalid binds are rejected with an error.
.TP
\fB\-w\fR, \fB\-\-workdir\fR \fIDIR\fR
Initial working directory inside the guest, as an absolute path (default /root).
.TP
\fB\-e\fR, \fB\-\-env\fR \fIKEY=VAL\fR
Pass an environment variable.
.TP
\fB\-c\fR, \fB\-\-cpus\fR \fIN\fR
CPU cores seen by the guest (0 = no limit).
.TP
\fB\-m\fR, \fB\-\-memory\fR \fISIZE\fR
RAM seen by the guest, e.g. 512M or 2G (0 = no limit).
.TP
.B \-\-hard\-mem
With \-\-memory, also enforce a hard cap via ulimit \-v.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-root\-id
Run as the host user instead of fake root.
.TP
.B \-\-trace
Debug syscall and seccomp tracing.
.RE
.TP
\fBset\fR \fINAME\fR [\fB\-c\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-m\fR \fISIZE\fR] [\fB\-\-hard\-mem\fR|\fB\-\-no\-hard\-mem\fR] [\fB\-\-reset\fR] [\fB\-\-show\fR]
Save default limits for a container.
.TP
.B list
List installed containers.
.TP
\fBfixperm\fR \fINAME\fR
Repair permissions and flush the library-path cache.
.TP
\fBremove\fR \fINAME\fR
Delete a container and its derived files.
.TP
.B source, ldsource
Print the C sources of the shim and the loader patcher.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B PRX_HOME
Data directory. Default: \fI~/.prx\fR if it exists, otherwise
\fI$XDG_DATA_HOME/prx\fR (\fI~/.local/share/prx\fR).
.TP
.B PRX_SRCDIR
Directory containing \fIprx.c\fR and \fIldpatch.c\fR.
.TP
.B CC
C compiler used by \fBprx build\fR.
.TP
.B PRX_QEMU
Path to a specific qemu-user binary for cross-architecture rootfs.
An i386 rootfs always needs qemu-i386 (there is no native i386 mode).
.TP
.B PRX_DNS
IPv4 and/or IPv6 nameservers to use inside the guest, separated by commas,
semicolons or blanks (a legacy colon-separated list of IPv4 addresses is still
accepted). Invalid entries and scoped addresses such as
.I fe80::1%eth0
are ignored with a warning.
.TP
.B PRX_KEEP
Set to 1 to leave guest processes running after
.B prx login
exits. By default they are terminated.
.TP
.B PRX_NO_ARGV0
Set to 1 to never pass
.B \-\-argv0
to the glibc loader. Normally this is detected automatically, because
.B \-\-argv0
exists only in glibc 2.33 and later; with an older rootfs the guest shell sees
its full path as argv[0].
.SH SECURITY NOTES
Inside the guest, PAM is configured to always succeed and sudo is configured
with NOPASSWD, because there is no real root to authenticate against. Do not
treat the guest as a sandbox for untrusted code.
.SH LIMITATIONS
Only glibc rootfs are supported (not musl). Only x86_64 and aarch64 hosts.
The loader patch depends on glibc's machine code and may not apply to every
version.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $PRX_HOME/rootfs/NAME
Container root filesystems.
.TP
.I $PRX_HOME/lib
Per-user built shim and patched loaders.
.TP
.I $PRX_HOME/conf/NAME.conf
Saved resource limits.
